<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Landgraviate of Leuchtenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1196 CE – 1646 CE (approx.) · capital Pfreimd</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Palatinate landgraviate whose Catholic landgraves ruled from Pfreimd until 1646, a title later revived for Napoleon's stepson Eugène de Beauharnais.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Leuchtenberg held a castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naab">Naab</a> valley in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a> and rose in the late 12th century, receiving the rank of landgrave in the 1190s. Their title gave them an unusual dignity for a modest territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> lands and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The landgraves ruled a small, rural territory and from 1332 resided at Pfreimd, where they later built a palace. They served the emperors and the Bohemian kings and sold or pledged lands to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>, so their domain shrank. The house remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, while much of the surrounding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a> turned Protestant.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last landgrave, Maximilian Adam, died in 1646, and Leuchtenberg passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In 1817 the King of Bavaria created the title Duke of Leuchtenberg for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-de-beauharnais">Eugène de Beauharnais</a>, whose descendants married into the Swedish, Portuguese and Russian royal families.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1196 CE – 1646 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.50%;width:8.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1196 CE – 1646 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pfreimd</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maximilian Adam of Leuchtenberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Leuchtenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1421&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-leuchtenberg" aria-label="Landgraviate of Leuchtenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4960 453 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Landgraviate of Leuchtenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5280 709L5229 694M5280 709L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5280 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5280 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pfreimd. <a href="/atlas?year=1421&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-leuchtenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
